**Read:** [Time-domain thermoreflectance — Jiang, Qian and Yang](../sources/Time-Domain-Thermoreflectance-Jiang-Qian-Yang.lingua#r_7ZHR7DG99WYCCG8J21HTKE9TMS), complete sensitivity, uncertainty and fitting developments; [Thermoreflectance methods — September 2026 review](../sources/Thermoreflectance-Methods-Review-2026.lingua#r_1XWEMH6TFMF988CCKE3YJ96RTW), complete common-model and complementary-method discussion; [FiPy — conservative finite-volume modelling](../sources/FiPy-Conservative-Finite-Volume-Methods.lingua#r_5C155DKGJ5WYGV18EJ1SC41G0Q), a checked forward diffusion model.

A forward model predicts observations from geometry, properties, boundaries and instrument response. An inverse problem estimates some of those quantities from observations. Heat diffusion smooths information, so recovering a rapid boundary history or a fine spatial property field can amplify noise severely.

Write the complete observation operator, including sensor averaging and timing. Examine sensitivity directions and parameter correlation before choosing an optimizer. Compare different initial estimates, parameterizations and independent datasets. A low residual can coexist with many incompatible parameter sets.

Regularization adds information or a preference, such as smoothness or bounded variation; it does not create measured detail. Justify its scale, test reconstruction on a withheld case and show how conclusions change with plausible noise and model discrepancy. Use the TDTR tutorial's full sensitivity treatment as a concrete example, including nuisance parameters and experimentally controllable depth sensitivity.

